186128962 1145 "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) with mass density ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/ca272ba1efbdf1cd3d7c6c0d41a91233ce4a64659e5c96d040f9ff2da45b6ddf.png) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/457c03e2f0a2295eaf404978491ea77c038507ba33bb1125a2a05952d0565af3.png)\n\nNotice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4c3142a8a4beae1e14a9ca2a611a7101d9a2a4cfe553109ae20a1d1b0dd78f64.png)\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d2a923f8537e18206a972cc41916ae6f9ee26abc93734cd0a73c50ffc165e08a.png) treating ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/0ea8d498dfe5ba44cab2ca474bceae4ef3205271d683d0fdeedf7ffd68a22a43.png) as a constant:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/a9b1d2210f33a1242fccf6517fbc63783381f19cdc48daf78685900497b0829f.png)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/73c3b5d4a287ad14dceb771dbd299c9effc9f3aff16c0f4b4051737ed99b36ae.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/3e75cffa3638e6260cbe5493a8277a484fe594d41e20ad55588da0b2dcf21fca.png)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/327bb4cc2df638a85e6bbe52eeda604b942f097b691c0c2125d579596dfe54a8.png)" 13194139535494 true] [17592186128962 1145 "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)xy^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^1 \\int_{0}^{x} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∫_{0}^{1}x[∫_{0}^{x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$" 13194139535494 false] [17592186128963 1100 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186128963 1100 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{(x,y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$." 13194139535494 false] [17592186128963 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nwith charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= ∬_(D)(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}[∫_{0}^{1}(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dx]dy &= ∫_{0}^{2}[4x - 3y^{2}x + x^{2}]_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(4(1) - 3y^{2}(1) + (1)^{2}) - (4(0) - 3y^{2}(0) - (0)^{2})]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy &= [5y - y^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (5(2) - (2)^{3}) - (5(0) - (0)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (10 - 8) - 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2$." 13194139535494 false] [17592186128963 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186128964 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186128964 1100 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2025/Source/Images/c3d92d5e0c81c1871fad053bd3726a50.png)" 13194139535494 false] [17592186128964 1145 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=9\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{4r\\cos θ}{\\sqrt{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{3}4r\\cos θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[∫_{0}^{3}rdr]dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{3}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ(\\frac{9}{2} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= 18∫_{π}^{3π/2}\\cos θdθ \\\\\n&= 18[\\sin θ]_{π}^{3π/2} \\\\\n&= 18[- 1 - 0] \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q =-18$." 13194139535494 false] [17592186128964 1145 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve $x^2+y^2=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\frac{9}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =18[-1-0] \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q = - 18.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186129143 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351888 13194139535494 true] [17592186129143 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351930 13194139535494 true] [17592186129143 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351937 13194139535494 true] [17592186129143 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351979 13194139535494 true] [17592186129144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351902 13194139535494 true] [17592186129144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351909 13194139535494 true] [17592186129144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351916 13194139535494 true] [17592186129144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351923 13194139535494 true] [17592186129144 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351944 13194139535494 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351895 13194139535494 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351951 13194139535494 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351958 13194139535494 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351965 13194139535494 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351972 13194139535494 true] [17592186129574 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186129574 1100 "A metal plate occupies the region $D = {(x, y): - 1 \\le y \\le 1, 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}}$. The charge density of the plate is given by $ρ(x, y) = xy^{2}$. Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." 13194139535494 false] [17592186129574 1100 "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." 13194139535494 true] [17592186129574 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/1cbe96aa4bf517302a2329db9f8eb2e9c56873e4d5a18cd5f1cd676543e97086.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}-(-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{1}{7}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351889 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1089 "q-38232" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2d6dc14c5296291bffa9a942ef9465b27477ee4c58550faba507703c2781a54b.png)\n\nA thin sheet of caramel occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=1,y=x$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the sheet is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ Find the total mass of the caramel sheet." 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1113 #uuid "5b6109e1-fa03-5ff2-889e-5d33063d5268" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351888 1145 "The mass of the caramel sheet occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $1\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/281ea7be1ef48c149bb4bcfd09aa41357caa8bb28adfc48285d298fb3cf89f3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4})-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the ice sheet is $M=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351890 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351891 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351892 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351893 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351894 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351894 13194139535494 true] [17592186351889 1110 #uuid "940140ba-78d4-56ab-8511-9d38235c80ca" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351890 1034 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351890 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351890 1111 #uuid "ddf19ee8-e4a9-58be-84e3-6035959466ce" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351891 1034 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351891 1111 #uuid "38113ef0-8c70-5c7a-af7f-0c0965e79e1f" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351892 1034 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351892 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351892 1111 #uuid "5f461ca6-0bc5-5010-884f-fc7855979560" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351893 1034 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351893 1111 #uuid "eb2ba121-7f6a-510a-bc53-41cfebe55de9" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351894 1034 "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351894 1111 #uuid "e8783cd7-ac91-5fab-9b41-88d5c1ae5bb4" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351896 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1089 "q-38942" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1100 "Find the mass of a glass object which has the form of an upper half-disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1113 #uuid "79fcdab8-3030-59a8-9b3c-1d84b6ba1d6f" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351895 1145 "The mass of an upper half-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/d8e2f73ce8840471143689748e188debaf5fde8f3aaf5021fd8d99c4f86a72d6.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}(-(-1)+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the upper half-disk is $M=\\frac{81}{2}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351897 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351898 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351899 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351900 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351901 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351901 13194139535494 true] [17592186351896 1110 #uuid "4be0b0c3-55af-5edf-8f4e-9598fd761675" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351897 1034 "\\frac{27}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351897 1111 #uuid "f6dc0b97-5075-553e-b8d4-e36f538457c7" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351898 1034 "\\frac{27}{8}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351898 1111 #uuid "b6ceeeb1-3c48-5548-926b-59b34347fa51" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351899 1034 "\\frac{81}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351899 1111 #uuid "05322158-3cce-5a59-a6a5-6b62e24c96d3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351900 1034 "\\frac{27}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351900 1111 #uuid "b010278b-ffdc-51bc-8384-2b154ac8dc56" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351901 1034 "\\frac{81}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351901 1111 #uuid "475c58c6-dbef-51a4-9119-09c6db559222" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351903 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1089 "q-94170" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1100 "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular wooden flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1113 #uuid "8f0d81d9-3cb8-57b4-8dc6-522b22cded6b" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351902 1145 "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=6{x}^{2}y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6y(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}32y\\,\\text{d}y & =[16{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot ({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=16.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351904 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351905 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351906 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351907 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351908 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351905 13194139535494 true] [17592186351903 1110 #uuid "19f77149-1c1c-5e30-9dc6-dce6454adaa6" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351904 1034 "12" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351904 1111 #uuid "b12f7cf6-6124-546d-bff9-8fae07230cb4" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351905 1034 "16" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351905 1111 #uuid "712689ff-6489-58aa-a834-7fc7e0e91cda" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351906 1034 "6" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351906 1111 #uuid "8b09075e-13b4-54ff-94fa-6565a2a6f034" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351907 1034 "1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351907 1111 #uuid "43ded51e-9d16-55fe-a5ed-26e1d86f956c" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351908 1034 "5" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351908 1111 #uuid "d936791a-4508-5918-9866-9747673d4277" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351910 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1089 "q-94174" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1100 "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular iron flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1113 #uuid "abc8b0fb-8221-56d4-b127-e2438d953bfb" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351909 1145 "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{6},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{6}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{6}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y & =[{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{4}-(0)^{4} \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=81.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351911 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351912 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351913 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351914 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351915 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351914 13194139535494 true] [17592186351910 1110 #uuid "ac5d4505-d90b-5e8a-acad-b3fb5fd5e63b" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351911 1034 "10" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351911 1111 #uuid "12212810-b7fa-5a0a-b7c7-9206c8e11cdb" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351912 1034 "50" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351912 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351912 1111 #uuid "c7996723-6436-563a-8cd6-44f034464fe2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351913 1034 "32" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351913 1111 #uuid "85d322b0-9eb7-552f-a546-eb999000fefc" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351914 1034 "81" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351914 1111 #uuid "e3550474-f463-5e06-9565-13b16f0939fb" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351915 1034 "5" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351915 1111 #uuid "874caefe-59e0-57b3-bf0e-60be4f171828" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351917 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1089 "q-94221" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1100 "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1113 #uuid "ff5e5f5f-7934-515a-b5b1-052ee590a017" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351916 1145 "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M=\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351918 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351919 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351920 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351921 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351922 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351920 13194139535494 true] [17592186351917 1110 #uuid "d0fb7e99-43ea-541b-bfe1-8623853d03f7" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351918 1034 "3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351918 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351918 1111 #uuid "888c450f-0b77-5a0b-887c-6d58590b4a93" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351919 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351919 1111 #uuid "1a0cae52-6494-5d86-9108-22e5163f8778" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351920 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351920 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351920 1111 #uuid "01abbb5b-dc08-5ed1-87d8-5e9a0fc3dd7f" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351921 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351921 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351921 1111 #uuid "944e6028-c801-5bc0-b3ed-dad5cdfb3ac9" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351922 1034 "4" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351922 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351922 1111 #uuid "525d7a3d-43db-5cb6-8a50-4fc5ca4c1da5" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351924 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1089 "q-94224" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1100 "The mass density function $\\lambda $ of a rectangular flat plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}.$ What is the total mass of the plate?" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1113 #uuid "6b393f80-2259-5a57-ae72-0a254617a38c" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351923 1145 "The mass $M$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:      \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{e}^{xy}]_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y & =[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-2)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the plate is $M={e}^{2}-3.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351925 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351926 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351927 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351928 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351929 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351925 13194139535494 true] [17592186351924 1110 #uuid "126335fd-eb30-522a-b418-fe0a7ca93cf9" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351925 1034 "{e}^{2}-3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351925 1111 #uuid "09dc945c-4250-5e07-9fe0-00a9c144f9a8" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351926 1034 "3-{e}^{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351926 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351926 1111 #uuid "f1176113-6d0e-52b9-b666-c6a47a666ee2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351927 1034 "{e}^{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351927 1111 #uuid "7fb2c1c6-8db1-5ad6-b1eb-839fc383afe3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351928 1034 "{e}^{3}-2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351928 1111 #uuid "9f7f650b-e53b-5bc6-9c48-27c6a0d6a5b5" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351929 1034 "e-3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351929 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351929 1111 #uuid "4ff905e1-2522-51f0-9dc9-fd577797a5be" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351931 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1089 "q-94389" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nAn iron flat plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=0,y=e,$ and $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}.$ Find the total mass of the plate." 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1113 #uuid "75df3b07-0877-5a7c-b6c0-1325b20ac75b" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351930 1145 "The mass of the plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. \n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =e \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/95a6d9b0bc464c719311dc900967ebd06a3aa3d3004cfe5303359eecb20551e7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{e}^{x}\\le y\\le e}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{x}}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the iron plate is $M=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351932 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351933 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351934 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351935 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351936 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351932 13194139535494 true] [17592186351931 1110 #uuid "0226aa79-f71f-5837-b61b-219722b3a652" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351932 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351932 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351932 1111 #uuid "c6ae9be1-ec4c-58bd-aa08-b389e49fdd51" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351933 1034 "2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351933 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351933 1111 #uuid "038a80b2-235f-59e9-850f-46eff9dc3150" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351934 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351934 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351934 1111 #uuid "78d9e1a7-2ff0-5a17-98e0-a64704f1cad4" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351935 1034 "1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351935 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351935 1111 #uuid "a46b1c88-b41f-54e7-95a3-5394f3380f48" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351936 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351936 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351936 1111 #uuid "7267e4d9-da8b-5530-a7cd-32ba51c56950" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351938 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1089 "q-94437" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1100 "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\pi },\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1113 #uuid "13b347ac-ad08-5860-805b-00e480d8cc18" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351937 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/2b179e35ae9af5a13d43c9a64bee99ee4ed87c257b623184f9165e14c24f3dee.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=1.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351939 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351940 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351941 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351942 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351943 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351941 13194139535494 true] [17592186351938 1110 #uuid "e9346e13-a39b-5300-9db8-743f5b792ca6" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351939 1034 "2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351939 1111 #uuid "bd41dfb6-e8c8-53f5-93ae-55acbcf6e65e" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351940 1034 "\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351940 1111 #uuid "3af58a3f-bdf4-5c33-81a5-81bd139eb32a" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351941 1034 "1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351941 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351941 1111 #uuid "9287031f-dd1d-5e59-bf58-59bd63a53c86" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351942 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351942 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351942 1111 #uuid "469a2a80-60d2-5811-b829-61b338fb8b5f" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351943 1034 "0" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351943 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351943 1111 #uuid "e95fd766-2362-5e60-b4cc-d626d7dc1c2b" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351945 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1089 "q-94535" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1100 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1113 #uuid "44761544-bc59-5004-b679-18f03588f956" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351944 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}{y}]}_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{\\pi }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351946 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351947 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351948 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351949 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351950 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351949 13194139535494 true] [17592186351945 1110 #uuid "d87f2432-7b26-59c3-b7f3-f64ea0421e53" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351946 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351946 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351946 1111 #uuid "ba9d50a0-d3d6-5b98-ad38-1986d41cce4a" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351947 1034 "2" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351947 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351947 1111 #uuid "0adc9b6b-20a9-55b1-83fd-6525cdef1ee3" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351948 1034 "\\pi +1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351948 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351948 1111 #uuid "cc28393e-8be9-5583-94ea-d56ce1d3832d" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351949 1034 "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351949 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351949 1111 #uuid "e85eee60-8b00-55ee-bae8-9cbdd9472101" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351950 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351950 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351950 1111 #uuid "aa1a399f-349c-524b-9246-05559e69bf1c" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351952 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1089 "q-94586" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7583a2da0bff7d66a3512c58f67692100af403b61b19a9b52347519b30823342.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed between  the parts of  the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ as shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1113 #uuid "550b89e0-e302-5d13-a797-fa0b28af8134" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351951 1145 "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a half-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radiuses $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\rho (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\rho (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}[r]_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\, \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\pi .$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351953 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351954 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351955 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351956 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351957 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351956 13194139535494 true] [17592186351952 1110 #uuid "f9362177-3c5b-5914-8f73-9e34645a2ecf" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351953 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351953 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351953 1111 #uuid "85cd58d8-459a-515a-b0d9-664fa8fad965" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351954 1034 "-\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351954 1111 #uuid "d827604d-a55d-560c-9cce-8e34787a78c1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351955 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351955 1111 #uuid "5f876516-3967-5257-a953-1f391002b77a" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351956 1034 "\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351956 1111 #uuid "e38925d6-a3f3-5a68-81cb-ab3b04459851" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351957 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351957 1111 #uuid "7a311b3d-ef6d-51a1-96bd-5af786b36880" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351959 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1089 "q-94720" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/514a3cd39f24a7b2477e6214964d69cd743a26645eba7f4b797af832b565fa93.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Find the mass of the object." 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1113 #uuid "49846630-f9bc-5f81-85c3-35e7a92041fb" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351958 1145 "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/7f3b2717946956ade07c252413130a730070e370cab85c73d866fa9506604106.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\frac{26}{3}.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351960 13194139535494 true] [17592186351959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351961 13194139535494 true] [17592186351959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351962 13194139535494 true] [17592186351959 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351963 13194139535494 t