{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351974 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351975 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351976 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351977 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351978 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351975 13194139535494 true] [17592186351973 1110 #uuid "1aa6e0e0-eaa7-5640-94fe-7f85275a03f9" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351974 1034 "20" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351974 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351974 1111 #uuid "d1676f25-de24-595f-9009-beefae456357" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351975 1034 "81\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351975 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351975 1111 #uuid "0c4d151f-a4c4-5144-aa5a-7f77a6305f72" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351976 1034 "50\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351976 1111 #uuid "15443454-96c2-5b24-9f52-1f2b4db0c8af" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351977 1034 "162\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351977 1111 #uuid "e5e93153-f54d-5207-ac15-07aad4c707d1" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351978 1034 "25\\pi " 13194139535494 true] [17592186351978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535494 true] [17592186351978 1111 #uuid "38b5e474-ec12-59b0-8e1b-328d44c82073" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351980 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1089 "q-95141" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1100 "Find the total charge $Q$ of the lower half of the circular disk ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=y$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1113 #uuid "b5bf2603-5b6f-5151-a667-e918275bd950" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351979 1145 "The total charge $Q$ of  the lower half of the circular disk  $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\le 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/af77552bd144fb70dfe8787060c0136f54955d20cdd2543391bd040b01a460d2.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-3\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/32db7009737aa47c8b5a96da91d72b0db95530271f463a9f7d35f715793cbea8.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}[{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}^{0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(0-\\frac{9-{x}^{2}}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({x}^{2}-9)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-9x]}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(9-27)-(-9+27)] \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the lower half of the circular disk is $Q=-18.$" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351981 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351982 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351983 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351984 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351985 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1041 "selection" 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351984 13194139535494 true] [17592186351980 1110 #uuid